  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; some tables are flattened in the supplied text.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study discusses safety alignment but tests continual-pretraining effects on toxicity, not harmful-request refusal/compliance, token-position KL, bypasses, safety localization, or safety-depth training.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports ToxiGen toxicity scores after continual pre-training, a related safety measure but not harmful-request compliance. It does not measure refusal rates or any of the other outcomes named in V1–V7. Toxicity results are recorded under versions [\"none\"].",
    "test_reason": "The study continually pre-trains Llama-2-7b-chat on 1B tokens of benign Traditional Chinese text. Its only safety-related measure is ToxiGen classifier toxicity of generations prompted with toxic or benign statements. It reports no harmful-request refusal or compliance rate, per-token KL, bypass attack, safety localization or deepening method. ToxiGen toxicity is a related but different construct from V2's harmful compliance, so the rows link to no version. The paper cites Qi et al. and its ethics statement says continual pre-training 'can compromise the models’ safety alignment', but it does not test this.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that forgetting during continual pre-training is non-trivial and not resolved by straightforward methods (layer or module freezing, adapters). Repetition worsens when models lean toward Traditional Chinese output. Knowledge is mainly unaffected while reliability (truthfulness, toxicity, bias) declines. Compromised safety alignment appears only in the ethics statement, as an untested concern for future work.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Continual pre-training on 1 billion tokens of general Traditional Chinese data, including freeze and adapter variants; not few-example harmful or benign supervised fine-tuning, and not a test of safety-depth methods.",
    "method_sensitivity": "ToxiGen toxicity scores vary between English and translated Traditional Chinese evaluations; the authors note particularly pronounced toxicity changes for Traditional Chinese after continual pre-training.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "After continual pre-training on benign Traditional Chinese text, ToxiGen toxicity on translated (TW) prompts rises from 0.22 (chat) to 3.15–7.97 for full and most partial-freeze variants. LoRA (3e-5) reaches 0.79. (IA)3 stays at 0.11–0.17. English toxicity stays at or below 0.08.",
        "proposed_claim": "Benign continual pre-training of an aligned model in a new language raises classifier-measured toxicity of its generations in that language, but not in English; tiny adapters such as (IA)3 avoid the rise.",
